// expect: answers f = 1
// why: the walk that discovers instantiations has to enter a lambda body, or a generic used only there is never monomorphised
// from: issues/lang/0295c

/**
 * `mapish<bool>` is named **once**, inside a lambda.
 *
 * Instantiations of a generic are discovered by walking every body — a call to a generic is the one
 * instance nothing declares — and that walk stopped at the lambda boundary. So this program named
 * an instance nothing recorded, nothing monomorphised, and `U` reached the emitter as *a value of a
 * type this emitter cannot write*.
 *
 * **The control is one line**: adding `Box<bool> top = b.mapish<bool>(…);` at statement level made
 * the same program compile, because then the instance was named somewhere the walk did reach. So
 * the instance emits perfectly well; what failed was *discovering* it. That is why this case has
 * exactly one mention of it, and why adding a second would stop it testing anything.
 *
 * `Pending<T>.map<U>` is the reachable form: mapping a ticket inside a `then` is the ordinary way
 * to write two dependent asynchronous steps, and every one of those met this.
 */
struct Box<T> {
  T v;
  Box<T> of(T v) { return Box<T>(v); }
  Box<U> mapish<U>(const this, fn[U(T)] f) { return Box<U>(f(this.v)); }
}
export i32 f() {
  Box<i32> b = Box.of(1);
  fn[bool()] c = () => b.mapish<bool>((i32 x) => x > 0).v;
  return c() ? 1 : 0;
}
